<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hōjō Tokimune</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1251–1284</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Hojo Tokimune</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hōjō Tokimune was the eighth regent (shikken) of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>, ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from 1268 to 1284. He rejected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a>'s demands for submission and led the defence against the Mongol invasions of 1274 and 1281, both repelled with the help of storms. A patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zen">Zen Buddhism</a>, he founded the Engaku-ji temple in Kamakura.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hojo-tokimune.jpg" alt="Hōjō Tokimune" width="320" height="618" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:H%C5%8Dj%C5%8D_Tokimune.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
